Z||000000||JasonS||11-07-2001||08:22 AM||||ok, so I can't complain, I have a drivable V8 Fiero and my neck is a little sore from being thrown back in the seat....but the frightening look of oncoming drivers when I swerve over in their lane needs to go.  The car launches like the space shuttle when you hit the gas, but it seems that when you take your foot off the car just wants to wonder everywhere...I can't blame it....it does own the road.... So what can I do to help it?  Shocks and struts seem to be ok.  I was thinking rear sway bar to start with?  any ideas?||||reg||6
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||11-07-2001||08:25 AM||||Hey Jason!<BR>Who did the conversion?<BR>Sounds like you need to juggle some weight from the rear to the front...<BR>Or add weight to the front...<BR>Was the suspention worked on/aligned?<BR>Did they put in poly engine cradle bushings?<BR>crash...<p>[This message has been edited by Fierowrecker (edited 11-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||JasonS||11-07-2001||09:00 AM||||Actually, myself and my cousin did the conversion (archie kit)ourselves.  It hasn't been aligned, which I will get done in the next few weeks, and everything is stock except the engine and brakes.  I remember Archie saying that he puts at least a rear sway bar on all the conversions that he does, will this help?  My cousin said the same thing as you did about putting more weight in the front, there's just not a lot of room up there to put anything since we did our own "custom" horizontal radiator mounting.<P>thanks for the help,<BR>Jason||||reg||1
Z||000003||Rodrv6||11-07-2001||09:07 AM||||It's probably the rear alignment.  If you have any toe out the car will be very unstable especially when decelerating or braking.  Be careful driving it until you get the alignment done.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  <BR>White 88 GT :)||||reg||4
Z||000004||Butter||11-07-2001||10:18 AM||||Jason,<BR>Definately line it up in the rear.  It has been my experience you cannot r and r the cradle without bringing it out of alinement.||||reg||1
Z||000005||JasonS||11-07-2001||10:36 AM||||Will definately do...thanks guys for the help<P>Jason||||reg||14
Z||000006||Archie||11-07-2001||10:38 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JasonS:<BR><B>Actually, myself and my cousin did the conversion (archie kit)ourselves.  It hasn't been aligned, which I will get done in the next few weeks, and everything is stock except the engine and brakes.  I remember Archie saying that he puts at least a rear sway bar on all the conversions that he does, will this help?  My cousin said the same thing as you did about putting more weight in the front, there's just not a lot of room up there to put anything since we did our own "custom" horizontal radiator mounting.<P>thanks for the help,<BR>Jason</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I don't necessarily use a sway bar on every swap. Many times on Customer cars we install a bar if the customer wants one. But on my own cars sometimes I put them on sometimes I don't.<P>I'll tell you what your problem is though.<P>You definitely need an ALIGNMENT ALIGNMENT ALIGNMENT.<P>You also may need poly "A" arm bushings. Many times the old rubber "A" arm bushings get "tired" and allow the "A" arms to move slightly North & South on acceleration and deceleration (especially in the rear). If that is happening just a wheel alignment can't correct it 100%. When the "A" arms move North & South, that causes the Toe In to momentarily change on heavy acceleration and deceleration.<P>So, IMHO, The most you MUST have is Poly Bushings & a WHEEL ALIGNMENT. If you already have good bushings, then you definitely need a WHEEL ALIGNMENT.<P>Archie<p>[This message has been edited by Archie (edited 11-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Formula88||11-07-2001||12:49 PM||||Great suggestion Archie.  I know someone in NC who has a Northstar Fiero and he said when you jump into it, when you suddenly lift off the throttle the rear end gets squirrely.  Sounds like alignment/poly needed there, too.  <P>Does that go for 88's as well, since the rear trailing arm setup is different?||||reg||6
Z||000008||cadero2dmax||11-09-2001||05:11 AM||||Sounds a lot like a big engine with the stock suspension. All that cast iron additional weight in the rear only accents the fearful Fiero bump steer. I didn't see which year Fiero you have, but suspect it is the 84 - 87 chassis.<P>If you want to increase the handling, there are a ton of different ways to go. Go to FieroRacingList@yahoogroups.com, and check their archives for shocks/ springs/ cradles/ bump steer kits/ alignment specs, etc. <P>These guys compete in autocross and road racing with their cars, they know how to make one drive!!<P>------------------<BR>G<BR>Daily Driver - <BR>85 SE<BR>4.9 Cad<BR>Isuzu 5 speed<BR>'88 cradle<BR>lowered 2 1/2"<BR>Mr. Mikes interior<P>GRM $2002 Challenge Fiero<BR>86 coupe<BR>4.9 (carburated)<BR>2.86:1 TH-125 w/shift kit||||reg||1
Z||000009||Philphine||11-09-2001||11:16 AM||||what dose the horizontal radiator help do? the chopper that i got from fiero dave has had that done and i've been wondering what the advantage is.||||reg||1
Z||000010||artherd||11-09-2001||06:58 PM||||ALIGNMENT ALIGNMENT ALIGNMENT!!!!!!!<P>Fierostore rear bar (or the hack-job relocation of a front bar-&gt; rear and a bigger front bar.)  DO NOT set up a car with the same bar front and rear.  It will oversteer like a mofo.<P>So yeah, replace all suspension bushings (don't forget your bushed *CRADLE* if it's not an 88.) <P><BR>And of course, your car is going to need a 4-wheel alignment!!! (rolls eyes)<P>Enjoy the V8, it's a real kick in the pants!!!!!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<BR><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000011||cadero2dmax||11-09-2001||08:29 PM||||I am sorry I had to hurry my response this morning - had to get to work.<P>First, forget swaybar. That has nothing to do with straightline directional changes with throttle lift (probably does it under acceleration too, but not as noticeably or as drastically). <P>Archie was correct, the first place to start is with bushings - again assuming you have an early chassis - and then alignment. If you have an '88, it is definitely an alignment problem. What specs to align to is the next <BR>decision. Stock specs will help, but not what you need. E-mail me privately, and I will give you some options for specs (depending on what you want to do with your car).<P>You will never eliminate all of the bump steer, or trailing throttle oversteer for that matter, with just alignment and bushings in an '84 - '87 chassis. The design is inherent to these handling characteristics.<BR>There are various ways to minimize it, but it cannot be eliminated. The best way to get a neutral handling 84 - 87 Fiero is to put an '88 cradle uner it. In the near future, a tubular cradle with a thoroughly modern racing suspension will be available, but for now the '88 is the only way to go.<P>Hope this helps a little, feel free to e-mail me if you need specifics.<P>G<P>------------------<BR>G<BR>Daily Driver - <BR>85 SE<BR>4.9 Cad<BR>Isuzu 5 speed<BR>'88 cradle<BR>lowered 2 1/2"<BR>Mr. Mikes interior<P>GRM $2002 Challenge Fiero<BR>86 coupe<BR>4.9 (carburated)<BR>2.86:1 TH-125 w/shift kit||||reg||1
